# How the check scripts and the deploy Makefile reach your board.
# Copy is placed as board.env; fill in the values you found in lesson 00.
# Syntax is shared by bash (the checks source it) and make (the Makefile includes it):
# NAME=value, no spaces around =, no quotes needed for simple values.
#
# The checks reach the board over key-based SSH (they never type a password), so lesson 00
# installs your public key on it. Choose ONE value for BOARD_HOST and use only that: SSH ties
# the accepted host key to the exact string, so the address and the .local name are two
# different records. The address is usually the steadier choice.
#
# The BOARD_HOST below is an example only; replace it with the address you found in lesson 00.
BOARD_HOST=192.168.1.50
BOARD_USER=root

# The board's interface names. WAN_IF is the upstream Ethernet interface: its name varies by
# board and image (eth0, end0, enp1s0, ...), so you DISCOVER yours in lesson 01 and record it
# here. Leave it blank until then; the checks that need it will say so. AP_IF is the Wi-Fi
# access-point interface, usually wlan0 — change it only if yours differs.
WAN_IF=
AP_IF=wlan0

# Optional: a host connected to the appliance's Wi-Fi, used by a few later checks
# (06 NAT, 07 management, and some optional lessons) to prove traffic from a real
# client. Leave blank until a lesson asks for it.
CLIENT_HOST=
CLIENT_USER=
